@node Protocol version
@section Protocol version

SSH 2.0 only.  An identification line that says 1.99 (which means
``compatible with SSH-1'') or 1.x is refused, and so is a line that does
not end in CR LF, contains a NUL or a byte outside printable ASCII, or is
over 255 bytes.  A client skips text a server sends before its line, up to
16 KiB; a server skips none.

@node Key exchange
@section Key exchange

@multitable @columnfractions .45 .55
@headitem Method @tab Where
@item @code{mlkem768x25519-sha256} @tab draft-ietf-sshm-mlkem-hybrid-kex
@item @code{sntrup761x25519-sha512} (and @code{@dots{}@@openssh.com}) @tab draft-ietf-sshm-ntruprime-ssh
@item @code{mlkem768nistp256-sha256}, @code{mlkem1024nistp384-sha384} @tab draft-ietf-sshm-mlkem-hybrid-kex
@item @code{curve25519-sha256} @tab RFC 8731
@item @code{ecdh-sha2-nistp256}, @code{ecdh-sha2-nistp384} @tab RFC 5656
@end multitable

The post-quantum hybrids come first in the default preference.  There is no
pure post-quantum method: a hybrid also holds if the post-quantum half were
broken.  Absent: every @code{diffie-hellman-group*} method and group
exchange (finite-field groups with negotiable or weak parameters, and the
Logjam family), all of SHA-1, @code{ecdh-sha2-nistp521},
@code{curve25519-sha256@@libssh.org} (the pre-RFC name), and GSS-API key
exchange.

The shared secret of a plain method is an mpint; of a hybrid, the hash of
the two secrets as a string, as the drafts say.  An X25519 result of all
zeros is refused on both sides (RFC 7748 section 6.1) and so is an ECDH
point that is not on its curve.

@node Host keys and signatures
@section Host keys and signatures

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .65
@headitem Algorithm @tab Rule
@item @code{ssh-ed25519} @tab A non-canonical S (not less than the group order) is rejected.
@item @code{ecdsa-sha2-nistp256}, @code{-nistp384} @tab The point must be uncompressed and on the curve.
@item @code{rsa-sha2-256}, @code{rsa-sha2-512} @tab Modulus 2048 to 16384 bits, odd; exponent odd and at least 65537.
@end multitable

Absent: @code{ssh-rsa} (RSA with SHA-1) and the SHA-1 signatures of the
@code{sk-} and certificate types, @code{ssh-dss}, ECDSA over P-521, Ed448,
and RSA keys under 2048 bits.  The key type name @samp{ssh-rsa} appears in
an RSA key's blob and is accepted there; the signature algorithm
@samp{ssh-rsa} is refused, and a signature's name must be the algorithm
that was negotiated.  OpenSSH certificates, security-key types and X.509
(RFC 6187) are not supported yet.

A client has no default trust in a host key.  The signature on the exchange
hash is verified first, then the application is asked through the
@code{host_key} callback; a client without one refuses every key.  At a
rekey the server must present the same key.

@node Ciphers
@section Ciphers

@code{chacha20-poly1305@@openssh.com}, @code{aes256-gcm@@openssh.com},
@code{aes128-gcm@@openssh.com}.  ChaCha20-Poly1305 is first: it hides the
packet length and does not depend on AES hardware.

Absent: every CBC and CTR cipher (CBC's padding oracle, and the
unauthenticated length that made Terrapin and the plaintext-recovery
attacks on SSH possible), RC4, 3DES, Blowfish, CAST, the @samp{none}
cipher, and all separate MACs including the encrypt-then-MAC ones.  The MAC
lists of KEXINIT are empty when sent and ignored when received.  Compression
is absent too (@samp{none} only): zlib before authentication was an attack
surface, and after it still leaks lengths.

@node Strict key exchange
@section Strict key exchange

The Terrapin attack (CVE-2023-48795) deletes or inserts packets in the
unauthenticated start of a connection.  OpenSSH's countermeasure, which
foxtrot @emph{requires}, has both sides declare it
(@code{kex-strict-c-v00@@openssh.com} and @code{kex-strict-s-v00@@openssh.com})
and then: the first packet each way must be KEXINIT; during the first key
exchange nothing but key exchange messages is accepted (so no IGNORE, DEBUG
or UNIMPLEMENTED to pad the transcript); and the sequence numbers restart at
zero at every NEWKEYS.  A peer that does not declare it is refused rather
than served without protection.

@node Rekeying
@section Rekeying

A connection rekeys after 1 GiB in either direction, 2^28 packets, or an
hour, whichever comes first (all configurable downwards or upwards), and
whenever the peer starts one.  The packet layer refuses to let a sequence
number wrap.  A key exchange that does not finish in 60 seconds ends the
connection.  Between a peer's KEXINIT and its NEWKEYS the peer may send
only transport and key exchange messages.  Application messages sent
during our own key exchange are held and sent after it, up to 1 MiB.

@node Authentication
@section Authentication
@cindex authentication, user

@file{auth.h} implements RFC 4252 and RFC 4256 as client and server
state machines that the application attaches to the transport: it hands
them the messages numbered 50 to 79 and they send theirs through a
callback.  The methods are @samp{none} (only to learn what is offered),
@samp{publickey}, @samp{publickey-hostbound-v00@@openssh.com},
@samp{password} and @samp{keyboard-interactive}.

@itemize @bullet
@item
A public key request is checked before the application sees it: the
encoding, the algorithm (one of @file{policy.h}; @samp{ssh-rsa} and the
rest are not there to be offered), that the key is one the policy accepts
and of the type the algorithm says, and the signature over the session
identifier.  The application only has to say whether it trusts the key for
the user.  A query (a request without a signature) asks only that.
@item
The host-bound method puts the server's host key in the signed data, so a
signature obtained for one server cannot be replayed to another through a
forwarding agent.  The server announces it in EXT_INFO
(@code{announce_hostbound} in the connection's configuration) and the
client then uses it; a request that names a different host key is refused.
@item
Password change is not supported: such a request fails without the
application seeing it.  Hostbased authentication is not implemented.
@item
A server ends the connection after six failed attempts (configurable); a
@samp{none} request and a public key query are not attempts.  It also ends
it if the user is not authenticated within 120 seconds, if the user name
or the service changes between requests, if a second request arrives
while the application is still deciding on the first, or if anything
else is malformed.  The user name is UTF-8 and at most 255 bytes.
@item
After success, further requests are ignored.
@item
The password is in the received packet only for the duration of the
callback, and the transport wipes its buffer after each packet; the
application should copy and wipe it as it needs.
@end itemize

The server's answers are asynchronous so that the application can ask a
database or another process: it answers a request now or later with
@code{gf_auth_server_accept}, @code{_reject}, @code{_pk_ok} or
@code{_kbdint_prompt}.  A client tries its keys in order, asking first
whether each would be accepted, then keyboard-interactive if it can answer,
then the password, within the methods the server says continue, and uses
the RSA hash the server lists in @code{server-sig-algs}.

@node The connection protocol
@section The connection protocol
@cindex channels
@cindex flow control

@file{chan.h} implements RFC 4254: session channels, @samp{direct-tcpip}
and @samp{forwarded-tcpip} channels, the requests on session channels
(@samp{exec}, @samp{shell}, @samp{subsystem}, @samp{pty-req}, @samp{env},
@samp{window-change}, @samp{signal}, @samp{exit-status}, @samp{exit-signal},
@samp{break}) and the global requests @samp{tcpip-forward},
@samp{cancel-tcpip-forward} and @samp{no-more-sessions@@openssh.com}.

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Flow control is strict and is the application's.}  The peer may
send only as much as the window we gave it.  More, or more in one message
than the packet size we announced, is a protocol error and ends the
connection.  The window is given back as the application says it has dealt
with the data (@code{gf_chans_consumed}), not as it arrives, so a slow
consumer slows the sender instead of filling memory.  Sending buffers
nothing: @code{gf_chans_send_data} takes what the peer's window and packet
size allow and says how much that was.
@item
The peer can do nothing on a channel that is not open: data, requests or
window changes for an unknown channel, for one that is still being opened
or for one it has closed, data after its own EOF, a window beyond
@math{2^32 - 1}, a confirmation of nothing or of a channel already open, a
reply to a request nobody made, and more unanswered requests or channels
than the configured limits (64 channels, 16 requests) all end the connection.
@item
@strong{What is refused.}  X11 forwarding and agent forwarding, in channels
and in requests: both extend the trust of the connection to other
credentials of the machine at the other end.  So is every channel type
other than the three above, and every request or global request the library
does not know, which is also how the @samp{keepalive@@openssh.com} request
is answered.  A refusal is sent in order behind the answers still owed for
earlier requests, as RFC 4254 requires.
@item
A channel number is reused only after the channel is closed on both sides.
A CLOSE is answered with a CLOSE; data in flight when a channel is closed
is dropped without a word to an application that has let go of it.
@item
A transport that cannot take a message during a key exchange (its queue
is full) makes @code{gf_chans_send_data} return @code{GF_ERR_AGAIN}; call
@code{gf_chans_resume} from the transport's @code{rekeyed} event.
@end itemize

@node SFTP
@section SFTP
@cindex SFTP

@file{sftp.h} speaks version 3 of the protocol
(draft-ietf-secsh-filexfer-02), the one every implementation speaks.  Later
drafts were never standardized and are not implemented; a peer that offers
nothing older than 3 is served, and one that cannot speak 3 is refused.  The
file system is the application's: a server gets each request parsed and
answers with a function; a client gets each response with the tag of its
request.

@itemize @bullet
@item
Packets are bounded (256 KiB by default) when the length can be read,
before the body arrives.  A client's requests and a server's unanswered
ones are bounded too, and so is the output kept for a peer that is not
reading.
@item
A client accepts only a response that fits its request: a READ is answered
with DATA or STATUS, never NAME, and DATA is never longer than the length
asked for.  A response for an id that is not outstanding, or one already
answered, ends the session.
@item
A server answers each request once and with a response that fits it; the
functions refuse anything else.  A malformed request that has an id is
answered with @samp{BAD_MESSAGE} and the session goes on; one that does not
ends it.
@item
@strong{A client refuses a directory listing with an empty name or a name
with a slash.}  A malicious server could otherwise name @file{../../.bashrc}
and have a naive recursive download write outside its target.  @samp{.} and
@samp{..} are left to the application, which has always had to skip them.
@item
@strong{Paths are not confined.}  That is the application's job, and it is
where the security of an SFTP server lies.  The interoperability server
resolves paths lexically under a root without leaving it; a real server
must also decide what to do about symbolic links.
@item
The target of SYMLINK comes first on the wire, as in OpenSSH, whatever the
draft's text says.
@end itemize

@node Other rules
@section Other rules

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{first_kex_packet_follows} is never set, and a peer that sets it is
refused: an optimistic guess is a key exchange message sent before
agreement.
@item
A user name or text field in a transport message must not contain NUL
(and, where RFC 4251 says UTF-8, must be valid UTF-8).
@item
Disconnect descriptions sent to the peer are fixed English text; they never
carry local detail.  Descriptions and debug text received from a peer are
untrusted and may contain terminal escape sequences.
@item
Key material is wiped after use, and so is each decrypted packet once
handled.  The library never logs.
@end itemize

@node Roadmap
@chapter Roadmap

Each step is tested against independent implementations before the next
one starts.

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Done.}  Status codes, wire types, policy, identification exchange,
the binary packet protocol for the three AEAD ciphers (OpenSSL known
answers), KEXINIT and negotiation, the seven key exchange methods with the
exchange hash and key derivation, Ed25519, ECDSA and RSA keys (OpenSSH and
OpenSSL known answers), and the transport state machine with rekeying,
EXT_INFO and the service request.  Verified against OpenSSH 9.6 in both
directions.

@item
@strong{Done.}  User authentication (RFC 4252, RFC 4256): public key with
the query and the host-bound variant, password and keyboard-interactive,
as client and server state machines.  Verified against OpenSSH 9.6 in both
directions, including rekeying after authentication.  Hostbased
authentication will not be implemented.

@item
@strong{Done.}  The connection protocol (RFC 4254): channels with strict flow
control, the requests of session channels, direct and forwarded TCP and
the global requests.  Verified against OpenSSH 9.6 in both directions:
commands, exit status, standard error, 8 MB through the windows with
rekeys every megabyte, and @command{ssh -L} and @command{ssh -R}.  Agent
and X11 forwarding are not planned: both extend the trust of the
connection to a machine's other credentials.

@item
@strong{Done.}  SFTP version 3 as client and server state machines over a
channel, with the file system left to the application.  Verified in both
directions against OpenSSH's @command{sftp} client and @samp{internal-sftp}:
20 MB each way with rekeys, listing, rename, links, permissions, and the
statuses of what fails.

@item
@strong{Done.}  A session object tying the transport, authentication and
channels together with one event interface; the interoperability programs
use it, and are some 200 lines shorter for it.

@item
OpenSSH certificates, FIDO security keys, passphrase-protected private key
files (which needs bcrypt-pbkdf, not in libgcrypt), a @file{known_hosts}
parser, and the X.509 formats of RFC 6187 through GnuTLS.
@end enumerate

@node Internationalization
@chapter Internationalization

Every string a person might read is wrapped in @code{_()} and looked up in
the @samp{foxtrot} domain; text sent to a peer is not translated.
@code{gf_crypto_init} binds the domain to the installed catalogues.

